<FoundationLayout
  numeral="IX"
  eyebrow="Foundation IX"
  title="The One Sent in His Name"
  tagline="Why a Yahuah verse fulfilled in the Son shows the Sender at work through the one He sent"
  description="Why a Yahuah verse fulfilled in the Son shows the Sender at work through the one He sent"
>

  <h2>The Argument in One Line</h2>

  <p>
    Many times the Apostolic writings take a verse that speaks of Yahuah in the Hebrew Scriptures and apply it to Yahushua. Joel says whoever calls on the name of Yahuah shall be delivered; Paul applies it to calling on the Master <em>Romans 10:13</em>. Isaiah says every knee shall bow to Yahuah; Paul says every knee shall bow at the name of Yahushua <em>Philippians 2:10–11</em>. Isaiah says Yahuah shall be a stone of stumbling; Peter says Yahushua is that stone <em>1 Peter 2:7–8</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The argument built on these verses is always the same. The Hebrew verse is about Yahuah. The apostle applies it to Yahushua. Therefore, it is said, Yahushua must be Yahuah. This piece answers every one of those passages at once, because they all rest on that single step.
  </p>

  <h2>A Contradiction Inside the Claim</h2>

  <p>
    Before looking at any of these verses, notice who Yahuah is in the Hebrew Scriptures. Isaiah prays, “thou, O LORD [Yahuah], art our father” <em>Isaiah 63:16</em>, and again, “O LORD [Yahuah], thou art our father” <em>Isaiah 64:8</em>. Malachi asks, “Have we not all one father? hath not one God created us?” <em>Malachi 2:10</em>. The Yahuah of the Hebrew Scriptures is the Father.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the step proves too much. If a Yahuah verse applied to the Son makes the Son Yahuah, then it makes the Son the Father. Yet the doctrine that makes this argument teaches the opposite. Its own creed says the person of the Father is one and the person of the Son is another, and the early church condemned as heresy the teaching that the Father and the Son are one and the same. The argument cannot be kept without breaking the creed it was brought in to defend.
  </p>

  <p>
    The usual escape is to say that the name Yahuah stands for all three together, not for the Father alone. But no verse in the Hebrew Scriptures says that. The prophets call Yahuah “our father”; they never call Him Father, Son and Spirit. The escape has to be carried in from the creed, because the text gives Yahuah one identity: the Father.
  </p>

  <p>
    Only one reading keeps both the prophets and the apostles true. The Son is not Yahuah. He is the one Yahuah sent, carrying Yahuah’s name.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Step Leaves Out</h2>

  <p>
    The step leaves out the most common pattern in all of Scripture: Yahuah acts through someone He sends. When the one sent acts, Yahuah is acting. When the one sent is received, Yahuah is received. When the one sent is rejected, Yahuah is rejected. None of this makes the one sent into Yahuah. It makes him Yahuah’s agent, carrying His name and His authority.
  </p>

  <div class="ref-word">
    <span class="ref-script" lang="he">שָׁלַח</span>
    <span class="ref-translit">shalach</span>
    <span class="ref-gloss">to send — the Hebrew verb behind “sent”; the one sent goes on another’s errand and in another’s name</span>
  </div>

  <h2>The Pattern in the Hebrew Scriptures</h2>

  <p>
    Yahuah told Israel, “Behold, I send an Angel before thee… obey his voice… for my name is in him” <em>Exodus 23:20–21</em>. The Angel carried Yahuah’s name. To disobey him was to disobey Yahuah. Yet he is plainly the one sent, not the One who sends.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the burning bush, “the angel of the LORD [Yahuah] appeared” <em>Exodus 3:2</em>, and then “God called unto him out of the midst of the bush” <em>Exodus 3:4</em>. The messenger stands in the bush, and Yahuah’s own words come through him.
  </p>

  <p>
    Yahuah even put His title on a man. He told Moses that Aaron would speak for him, and “thou shalt be to him instead of God” <em>Exodus 4:16</em>. Later He said, “I have made thee a god to Pharaoh” <em>Exodus 7:1</em>. Moses stood before Pharaoh in Yahuah’s place, and what Moses did, Yahuah did. Moses was still a man.
  </p>

  <p>
    And Moses foretold the greatest sent one of all. Yahuah promised to raise up a Prophet like Moses: “I will put my words in his mouth… my words which he shall speak in my name” <em>Deuteronomy 18:18–19</em>. The coming Prophet would speak Yahuah’s words, in Yahuah’s name. That is the description of the Son.
  </p>

  <h2>The Son Says He Came in Another’s Name</h2>

  <p>
    Yahushua described Himself in exactly these terms. “I am come in my Father’s name” <em>John 5:43</em>. “the works that I do in my Father’s name” <em>John 10:25</em>. “I have not spoken of myself; but the Father which sent me, he gave me a commandment, what I should say” <em>John 12:49</em>. To His Father He said, “I kept them in thy name” <em>John 17:12</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the crowds cried “Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord” <em>Matthew 21:9</em> <em>John 12:13</em>, they were quoting Psalm 118:26, where the one blessed comes “in the name of the LORD [Yahuah].” The verse itself puts two in view: the one who comes, and the One whose name he comes in.
  </p>

  <p>
    So when a Yahuah verse is fulfilled in the Son, the Son is doing what He said He came to do: Yahuah’s work, in Yahuah’s name. The Father is at work in Him <em>John 14:10</em>. The verse does not move from the Father to the Son. It runs from the Father, through the Son.
  </p>

  <h2>The Believer Carries the Name Too</h2>

  <p>
    The plainest test is to ask whether Scripture says the same things about those the Son sends. It does.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Master told His disciples, “He that receiveth you receiveth me, and he that receiveth me receiveth him that sent me” <em>Matthew 10:40</em>. “He that heareth you heareth me” <em>Luke 10:16</em>. The same chain of sending runs from the Father to the Son to the disciples. If receiving the Son meant the Son is the Father, then receiving the disciples would mean the disciples are the Son.
  </p>

  <p>
    The disciples cast out devils “through thy name” <em>Luke 10:17</em>. Paul was a chosen vessel “to bear my name before the Gentiles” <em>Acts 9:15</em>. The overcomer will have “the name of my God” written on him <em>Revelation 3:12</em>. Carrying someone’s name, acting in it and being received for it is what a sent one does. The believer does it for the Son, and the Son does it for the Father.
  </p>

  <h2>Read the Next Line</h2>

  <p>
    In nearly every place where a Yahuah verse is applied to the Son, the apostle names the Father in the same breath, and the Father is the one in charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paul applies Joel’s “call on the name” to the Master, but four verses earlier he tells the reader to believe “that God hath raised him from the dead” <em>Romans 10:9</em>. The one called on was raised by God. He is not the God who raised Him.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paul applies Isaiah’s “every knee shall bow” <em>Isaiah 45:23</em> to the Son, but he begins, “God also hath highly exalted him, and given him a name” <em>Philippians 2:9</em>, and he ends, “to the glory of God the Father” <em>Philippians 2:11</em>. The bowing is given to the Son by God, and it ends in glory to the Father. The one exalted is not the One who exalted Him.
  </p>

  <p>
    Peter calls Yahushua the stone of stumbling, but he quotes Isaiah first: “Behold, I lay in Sion a chief corner stone” <em>1 Peter 2:6</em>. In Isaiah, Yahuah is the one who lays the stone <em>Isaiah 28:16</em>. The builder is not the stone he lays. Yahuah becomes a stumbling stone to Israel by the stone He set in place, and the psalm says of it, “This is the LORD’s [Yahuah’s] doing” <em>Psalm 118:22–23</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Asymmetry</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the pattern that settles the question. Yahuah verses are applied to the Son. But Scripture never turns it around. The Father is never sent by the Son. The Father never comes in the Son’s name. The Father is never raised, exalted or given a name by the Son. Every arrow runs one way: from the Father, to the Son, and through the Son to the people.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the Son were Yahuah in the same sense the Father is, the arrows would sometimes point back. They never do. That is what a sent one looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>The Test for Every Yahuah Verse Applied to the Son</h2>

  <p>
    Every such verse can be read with three plain questions. First, does the passage say the Son was sent, given or exalted? Second, does the apostle name the Father nearby as the one who raised, exalted or sent Him? Third, does Scripture say the same kind of thing about the believers the Son sends? In every case the answer is yes.
  </p>

  <p>
    A Yahuah verse fulfilled in the Son shows that the Son came in the Father’s name and did the Father’s work. It does not show that the Son is the Father, or a second Yahuah. The name He carries is His Father’s, and He carries it to us.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the end, those who follow the Lamb stand with Him on Mount Sion, “having his Father’s name written in their foreheads” <em>Revelation 14:1</em>. The Son brings His people home carrying the very name He came in — the name of the Father who sent Him.
  </p>

